Given a (year, month, day) date, the function has to return the next calendar day as a tuple, rolling over months, years and leap-day Februaries correctly.

After unpacking the date and defining a table of month lengths with February at 28, the submission does this:

    def is_leap(y):
        return y % 400 == 0 or (y % 4 == 0 and y % 100 != 0)
    last_day = 29 if (month == 2 and is_leap(year)) else days_in_month[month]
    if day + 1 <= last_day:
        return (year, month, day + 1)
    if month == 12:
        return (year + 1, 1, 1)
    return (year, month + 1, 1)

The leap test is the Gregorian one, and it only matters for February, where it raises the last day to 29. If the day can be incremented without passing the month's last day, it is. Otherwise the date moves to the first of the following month, and December wraps to January 1 of the next year.

The three examples involve January and December of 2021, so February and leap years are untested by them. I traced next_day((2024, 2, 28)): 2024 is a leap year, last_day is 29, and the result is (2024, 2, 29). From (2024, 2, 29), incrementing would exceed 29, so it returns (2024, 3, 1). For (1900, 2, 28), 1900 is not a leap year under the century rule, so the answer is (1900, 3, 1), which is correct. (2000, 2, 28) goes to (2000, 2, 29), and (2023, 4, 30) rolls to (2023, 5, 1). These all match the calendar. A date in the middle of a month, such as (2022, 7, 14), simply advances to (2022, 7, 15).

