The function must return, in ascending order, all positive divisors of a non-negative integer n that are strictly smaller than n.

The submission finds divisors in pairs up to the square root:

    while i * i <= n:
        if n % i == 0:
            j = n // i
            if i != n:
                small.append(i)
            if j != i and j != n:
                large.append(j)

Each i from 1 while i * i <= n is tested; when it divides n, both i and its partner n // i are divisors. The small one is kept unless it equals n, and the large one is kept unless it duplicates i (a perfect-square root) or equals n itself. Inside the loop i then advances by one; after the loop, large is sorted and the function returns small + large. Every small value is at most the square root and every large value at least it, so concatenating the two ascending lists yields a sorted result.

The examples cover 12, 28 and the prime 7. I checked 36, a perfect square: the pairs give small values 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and large values 18, 12, 9, with the partner of 6 skipped because it equals 6 and the partner of 1 skipped because it is 36. The output is [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18], which is correct. For n = 1 the only candidate equals n, so it returns []. For n = 0 the loop never runs and it returns [], consistent with there being no positive number below 0. A prime such as 13 returns [1]. The opening abs(n) is unnecessary for non-negative input but harmless.

Verdict: PASS
